//! Format: MPEG-4 Part 2 (Simple and Advanced Simple Profile) decoder component.
//! Supported: Rectangular 8-bit 4:2:0 video object layers: I, P and B video object planes, half-sample motion, four motion vectors a macroblock, unrestricted vectors, intra AC/DC prediction, and both the H.263 and MPEG quantisers.
//! Not supported: Quarter-sample motion, global motion compensation, interlacing, data partitioning, reversible codes, sprites, and arbitrary object shapes.
//! Role: This file owns the normative variable length code tables of ISO/IEC 14496-2, the scan orders, and the default quantiser matrices, plus the decoding trees a test proves equivalent to them.
//
// @LICENCE: MIT
// @ORIGIN: oxideav-mpeg4video, https://github.com/OxideAV/oxideav-mpeg4video
// @MODIFIED: The normative tables of ISO/IEC 14496-2 were recovered from that project's own
// transcription of the standard and rewritten in the form this decoder reads. Every table was
// re-checked here: each is prefix-free, its Kraft sum does not exceed one, and its entries are
// distinct. No code of that project is reproduced.
// @NOTICE: /bin/THIRDPARTY.md#mpeg-4-part-2-normative-tables
#global namespace Mpeg4
#global internal

// Everything here is fixed by the format: a changed value changes what the decoder decodes.
//
// Each variable length code is stated twice. `<Name>Len` and `<Name>Cod` are the normative form -
// the length and the value of every code word, in the order the standard lists them - and
// `<Name>Tree` is the same information as a binary tree, which is what the decoder walks. A
// positive slot of a tree is a child node, a negative slot is minus one more than the entry it
// reaches, and a zero slot is a bit pattern the table does not define. `mpeg4.tables.test.swg`
// walks every normative code word through its tree and requires them to agree.

// Table B.6, `mcbpc` of an intra plane. Type five is the stuffing code, which carries nothing.
